```cpp
void replaceOrAppend(vector<int>& nums, int target, int replacement) {
    // Find the first occurrence of the target integer in the vector
    auto it = find(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target);
    if (it != nums.end()) {
        // Replace the found element with the replacement value
        *it = replacement;
    } else {
        // Append the replacement value to the end of the vector
        nums.push_back(replacement);
    }
}
```
This implementation uses the `std::find` algorithm to find the first occurrence of the target integer in the vector, and then replaces it with the replacement value if found, or appends the replacement value to the end of the vector otherwise.

To optimize for both time and space complexity, we can use a hash-based lookup instead of linear search to find the first occurrence of the target integer in the vector. This would reduce the time complexity from O(n) to O(1), where n is the size of the vector. Here's an example implementation that uses a hash table to store the indices of the elements in the vector:
```cpp
