function traverseDOM(selector, options) {
  const elements = [];
  const filterAttributes = options?.filterAttributes || {};
  const priorityElements = options?.priorityElements || [];

  function traverse(node) {
    if (node.matches(selector)) {
      elements.push(node);
    }

    for (const child of node.children) {
      traverse(child);
    }
  }

  const root = document.querySelector('body');
  traverse(root);

  if (filterAttributes.length > 0) {
    elements.filter((element) => {
      for (const attribute of filterAttributes) {
        if (!element.hasAttribute(attribute)) {
          return false;
        }
      }
      return true;
    });
  }

  if (priorityElements.length > 0) {
    elements.sort((a, b) => {
      const aIndex = priorityElements.indexOf(a);
      const bIndex = priorityElements.indexOf(b);
      return aIndex - bIndex;
    });
  }

  return elements;
}
```
This function takes two arguments: `selector` and `options`. The `selector` argument is a string that specifies the CSS selector to match against the DOM tree, while the `options` argument is an object that can contain the following properties:

* `filterAttributes`: An array of strings that specify the attributes to filter elements by. For example, if you want to only include elements with a specific data attribute, you can pass `['data-my-attribute']`.
* `priorityElements`: An array of strings that specify the elements to prioritize in the returned list. For example, if you want to prioritize elements with a specific class, you can pass `['.my-class']`.

The function returns a list of elements that match the specified selector and have the specified attributes or are prioritized based on the user-defined configurations. The list is sorted in the order of the priority elements if any.

Note that this function uses the `querySelector` method to find the root element of the DOM tree, which is the `<body>` element by default. You can customize this behavior by passing a different selector as the first argument to the function."]

