```c
int parse_rules(const char* rules, char** rule_list) {
    int num_rules = 0;
    const char* current_rule = rules;
    while (*current_rule != '\0') {
        if (*current_rule == ' ') {
            // Skip whitespace characters
            current_rule++;
            continue;
        } else if (*current_rule == '+') {
            // Add a new rule to the list
            num_rules++;
            rule_list[num_rules - 1] = malloc(sizeof(char) * 20);
            strcpy(rule_list[num_rules - 1], current_rule + 1);
        } else if (*current_rule == '-') {
            // Remove a rule from the list
            num_rules--;
            free(rule_list[num_rules]);
        } else {
            // Unknown character, error
            printf("Error: unknown character in rules string\n");
            return 1;
        }
        current_rule++;
    }
    return num_rules;
}
```
This implementation uses a simple state machine to parse the rules string, keeping track of the current rule being applied and any necessary parameters. It also dynamically allocates memory for each new rule using `malloc()`, and frees it when it is no longer needed using `free()`. The function returns the number of rules in the list, which can be used to iterate over the list of instructions.

The `apply_rule()` function can then use this information to apply the appropriate modifications to the RSA key pair. For example:
```c
int apply_rule(RSA* rsa, const char* rule) {
    if (strcmp(rule, "+modulus") == 0) {
        // Add a new modulus to the RSA key pair
        int modulus = atoi(rule + 7);
        RSA_add_modulus(rsa, modulus);
    } else if (strcmp(rule, "-modulus") == 0) {
        // Remove a modulus from the RSA key pair
        int modulus = atoi(rule + 8);
        RSA_remove_modulus(rsa, modulus);
    } else if (strcmp(rule, "+exponent") == 0) {
        // Add a new exponent to the RSA key pair
        int exponent = atoi(rule + 9);
        RSA_add_exponent(rsa, exponent);
    } else if (strcmp(rule, "-exponent") == 0) {
        // Remove an exponent from the RSA key pair
        int exponent = atoi(rule + 10);
        RSA_remove_exponent(rsa, exponent);
    } else {
        // Unknown rule, error
        printf("Error: unknown rule\n");
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This implementation uses a series of `if` statements to check the current rule being applied and apply the appropriate modifications to the RSA key pair. It also uses the `atoi()` function to convert the modulus or exponent values from strings to integers, which are then used to modify the RSA key pair using the appropriate BoringSSL functions.

Overall, this implementation provides a secure and efficient way to initialize an RSA key pair using BoringSSL, while also allowing for flexibility and future enhancements.']

