The function optimizes performance by considering cache locality and minimizing lock contention, especially under high concurrency scenarios. It efficiently processes configuration updates in batches, particularly for those involving large-scale deployments or complex dependency graphs, mitigating the impact of network latency and I/O bottlenecks. The function also implements a caching mechanism to improve performance and extend the data structures to support additional fields such as deployment environments and service tags.

The function is designed to seamlessly integrate with existing configuration management pipelines and support easy extension. It considers the trade-offs between synchronous and asynchronous I/O models for optimal performance under different load conditions. Additionally, it dynamically adjusts the configuration update interval based on system metrics and resource availability, ensuring efficient handling of nested dependencies and detection of circular references.

Within the same function, a switch statement is used to determine the service type and associated role-based access controls. The function uses library functions to retrieve the service type and another library function to obtain the access controls, returning these details as part of the configuration metadata. The function also handles potential I/O errors during configuration retrieval, retrying failed operations and providing detailed error reporting.

The function is integrated with a distributed configuration framework to ensure centralized management across multiple instances. It ensures the function can operate under resource constraints, such as limited CPU and memory, by implementing efficient algorithms and resource management techniques. The function provides mechanisms for users to configure default settings and override them at runtime. Additionally, it considers the impact of data locality and disk caching strategies on performance, optimizing for read-heavy workloads.

Finally, the function detects and handles circular dependencies in configuration graphs, ensuring data integrity and avoiding infinite loops. It also employs batch processing and parallel processing where appropriate to ensure it scales well under increasing data volumes.']

