This implementation includes a custom caching strategy with a fixed size and LRU replacement policy, as well as rate limiting based on user IP addresses and query patterns. It also includes backoff logic to prevent overloading the DNS server during peak loads. The system is designed to be resilient against distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks, employing traffic shaping and anomaly detection to differentiate between legitimate and malicious traffic patterns.

The custom caching strategy with a fixed size and LRU replacement policy ensures that the cache is efficient in terms of memory usage while still providing good hit rates and cold start latency. The rate limiting mechanism helps prevent abuse by limiting the number of queries per user IP address or query pattern, while dynamically adjusting limits based on real-time load and historical data. The backoff logic prevents overloading the DNS server during peak loads, while also providing a mechanism for retrying failed queries with exponential backoff.

The system is designed to be modular, facilitating easy updates and integration with future name resolution services. It also includes logging of successful queries and failures, providing detailed metrics for monitoring query throughput, latency, and cache hit rates. The system is also resilient against malformed DNS responses and network partitioning scenarios, minimizing disruptions to users.

Overall, this implementation provides a scalable and efficient DNS resolution utility in C that supports asynchronous query handling, custom caching strategies, rate limiting, backoff logic, and logging of successful queries and failures. It is designed to be resilient against distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks and can handle multiple query types, including SRV, MX, and CNAME, each with its own priority and caching policy.']

