Here is an example of how you could implement the high-throughput Java module responsible for processing a continuously streaming dataset of user transactions:
```
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class TransactionProcessor {
  private static final int MAX_THREADS = 10;
  private static final long MIN_IDLE_TIME = TimeUnit.SECONDS.toMillis(30);
  private static final long MAX_IDLE_TIME = TimeUnit.MINUTES.toMillis(5);

  private ConcurrentHashMap<String, Transaction> transactions;
  private ExecutorService executor;

  public TransactionProcessor() {
    this.transactions = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(MAX_THREADS);
  }

  public void processTransaction(Transaction transaction) {
    String key = transaction.getKey();
    transactions.putIfAbsent(key, transaction);
    executor.execute(() -> {
      try {
        // Perform discrepancy check and update transaction status
        if (isDiscrepant(transaction)) {
          transaction.setStatus("DISCREPANT");
        } else {
          transaction.setStatus("VALID");
        }
        transactions.put(key, transaction);
      } catch (Exception e) {
        // Handle exception
      }
    });
  }

  public void flush() {
    executor.shutdown();
    try {
      if (!executor.awaitTermination(MAX_IDLE_TIME, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
        executor.shutdownNow();
      }
    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
      // Handle exception
    }
  }

  private boolean isDiscrepant(Transaction transaction) {
    // Implement discrepancy check logic here
    return false;
  }
}
```
This implementation uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` to store the transactions and an `ExecutorService` to process them concurrently. The `processTransaction()` method adds the transaction to the map if it doesn't already exist, and then submits a task to the executor service to perform the discrepancy check and update the transaction status. The `flush()` method shuts down the executor service when it is idle for more than 5 minutes or when the system is shutting down.

