Here is an example of how you could implement a distributed, resilient cache using Java:
```
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class DistributedCache {
  private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(DistributedCache.class.getName());

  // Map to store the cache entries
  private ConcurrentHashMap<String, CacheEntry> cacheMap;

  // Atomic integer to track the number of hits and misses
  private AtomicInteger hitCount = new AtomicInteger();
  private AtomicInteger missCount = new AtomicInteger();

  // Eviction policy for the cache
  private EvictionPolicy evictionPolicy;

  public DistributedCache(EvictionPolicy evictionPolicy) {
    this.evictionPolicy = evictionPolicy;
    cacheMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
  }

  public void put(String key, CacheEntry value) {
    // Check if the key already exists in the cache
    if (cacheMap.containsKey(key)) {
      // If it does, update the existing entry with the new value
      cacheMap.put(key, value);
    } else {
      // If it doesn't, add a new entry to the cache
      cacheMap.put(key, value);
    }
  }

  public CacheEntry get(String key) {
    // Check if the key exists in the cache
    if (cacheMap.containsKey(key)) {
      // If it does, return the corresponding entry
      return cacheMap.get(key);
    } else {
      // If it doesn't, return null
      return null;
    }
  }

  public void evict(String key) {
    // Check if the key exists in the cache
    if (cacheMap.containsKey(key)) {
      // If it does, remove the entry from the cache
      cacheMap.remove(key);
    }
  }

  public void clear() {
    // Clear the entire cache
    cacheMap.clear();
  }

  public int getHitCount() {
    return hitCount.get();
  }

  public int getMissCount() {
    return missCount.get();
  }
}
```
The `DistributedCache` class is a simple cache that stores key-value pairs in a `ConcurrentHashMap`. It also maintains two atomic counters to track the number of hits and misses. The `put` method updates an existing entry if it already exists, or adds a new entry if it doesn't. The `get` method returns the corresponding value for a given key, or null if the key doesn't exist. The `evict` method removes an entry from the cache if it exists. The `clear` method clears the entire cache.

The `EvictionPolicy` interface defines the methods that need to be implemented by any eviction policy:
```
public interface EvictionPolicy {
  public void onPut(String key, CacheEntry value);
  public void onGet(String key);
  public void onRemove(String key);
}
```
The `onPut` method is called whenever a new entry is added to the cache. The `onGet` method is called whenever an existing entry is retrieved from the cache. The `onRemove` method is called whenever an entry is removed from the cache.

Here's an example of how you could implement a simple eviction policy that removes the least recently used entry when the cache is full:
```
public class LeastRecentlyUsedEvictionPolicy implements EvictionPolicy {
  private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(LeastRecentlyUsedEvictionPolicy.class.getName());

  // Map to store the cache entries and their access times
  private ConcurrentHashMap<String, CacheEntry> cacheMap;

  public LeastRecentlyUsedEvictionPolicy() {
    cacheMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
  }

  @Override
  public void onPut(String key, CacheEntry value) {
    // Update the access time for the entry
    cacheMap.put(key, value);
  }

  @Override
  public void onGet(String key) {
    // Update the access time for the entry
    cacheMap.get(key).setAccessTime(System.currentTimeMillis());
  }

  @Override
  public void onRemove(String key) {
    // Remove the least recently used entry from the cache
    CacheEntry leastRecentlyUsed = null;
    for (CacheEntry entry : cacheMap.values()) {
      if (leastRecentlyUsed == null || entry.getAccessTime() < leastRecentlyUsed.getAccessTime()) {
        leastRecentlyUsed = entry;
      }
    }
    cacheMap.remove(leastRecentlyUsed.getKey());
  }
}
```
The `LeastRecentlyUsedEvictionPolicy` class maintains a map of cache entries and their access times. Whenever an entry is added or retrieved from the cache, its access time is updated. When the cache is full and an entry needs to be removed, the least recently used entry is selected for removal based on its access time.

To test the cache, you could use a testing framework like JUnit to write unit tests that simulate real-world usage patterns and stress tests the cache under various conditions. Here's an example of how you could write a unit test for the `DistributedCache` class:
```
import org.junit.Test;
import static org.junit.Assert.*;

public class DistributedCacheTest {
  @Test
  public void testPut() {
    // Create a new cache with an eviction policy
    DistributedCache cache = new DistributedCache(new LeastRecentlyUsedEvictionPolicy());

    // Add some entries to the cache
    cache.put("key1", "value1");
    cache.put("key2", "value2");
    cache.put("key3", "value3");

    // Check that the cache contains the expected entries
    assertEquals(cache.get("key1"), "value1");
    assertEquals(cache.get("key2"), "value2");
    assertEquals(cache.get("key3"), "value3");
  }

  @Test
  public void testGet() {
    // Create a new cache with an eviction policy
    DistributedCache cache = new DistributedCache(new LeastRecentlyUsedEvictionPolicy());

    // Add some entries to the cache
    cache.put("key1", "value1");
    cache.put("key2", "value2");
    cache.put("key3", "value3");

    // Check that the cache returns the expected values for each key
    assertEquals(cache.get("key1"), "value1");
    assertEquals(cache.get("key2"), "value2");
    assertEquals(cache.get("key3"), "value3");
  }

  @Test
  public void testEvict() {
    // Create a new cache with an eviction policy
    DistributedCache cache = new DistributedCache(new LeastRecentlyUsedEvictionPolicy());

    // Add some entries to the cache
    cache.put("key1", "value1");
    cache.put("key2", "value2");
    cache.put("key3", "value3");

    // Check that the cache contains the expected entries
    assertEquals(cache.get("key1"), "value1");
    assertEquals(cache.get("key2"), "value2");
    assertEquals(cache.get("key3"), "value3");

    // Evict an entry from the cache
    cache.evict("key2");

    // Check that the cache no longer contains the evicted entry
    assertNull(cache.get("key2"));
  }
}
```
The `testPut` method tests that the `put` method adds new entries to the cache correctly. The `testGet` method tests that the `get` method returns the expected values for each key. The `testEvict` method tests that the `evict` method removes an entry from the cache correctly.

